/* =========================================================================
   BEHAVIOR — Chris Roe's behavior series. The grammar underneath the calls:
   how elk actually communicate, and what that means for setups and calling.
   ========================================================================= */
const BEHAVIOR = [
  {
    id:"see-you-first",
    tag:"Behavior series · principle 1",
    title:"See you first, hear you second, smell you third",
    summary:"Elk communicate visually before anything else. When you call, you’re promising a set of eyes — so set up where they’ll naturally come looking, and take the downwind option away from them.",
    sections:[
      { h:"The three ways elk talk", body:"Elk are herd animals adapted to open country, so day-to-day communication is mostly <b>visual</b> — body language, ear position, head and neck position, read across the group.<ul><li><b>Vocalizing</b> kicks in when sight isn’t possible, isn’t telling them enough, or when they want to back up what their eyes are seeing.</li><li><b>Smell</b> comes last for <i>communication</i> — scent-checking a cow for estrus, a cow confirming a calf is hers, or working out something the eyes and ears can’t make sense of.</li></ul>The livestock version: cattle and horses left alone are quiet and in sight of each other. Move them between pastures, sort the calves, pull one horse out — <i>now</i> they’re bawling. Elk are no different." },
      { h:"One important caveat", body:"“Smell you third” is about how elk <b>prioritize communication</b> — <b>not</b> how important their senses are. For sorting out danger, their nose trumps everything. <b>They can be fooled by their eyes and fooled by their ears; they are rarely fooled by their nose.</b>" },
      { h:"What it means when you call", body:"Every time you call you’re playing an elk engaging in <b>vocal</b> communication — which tells any elk in earshot exactly what you want, what you’re doing, and roughly where you are. And if he’s interested, he expects that at some point he’ll <b>see</b> the animal he’s hearing. Sometimes he’ll come make that contact; sometimes (a lost mew, say) he expects <b>you</b> to come to <b>him</b>. Either way, expect him to want eyes on you as soon as possible — and if you’re the one who should be closing the distance and you don’t, within about the time he’d expect it, that’s when things fall apart." },
      { h:"Setting up so they come looking", body:"“Set up back in cover, not on the edge of an opening” is good advice — but <b>setting up in heavy cover and setting up in the spot within that cover where an elk naturally wants to make visual contact are two different things.</b> Pick the spot that <i>he</i> would choose as the place to lay eyes on you. Then pick vocalizations that make coming to look feel like his idea. You can never make an elk do anything — all you can do is put in his mind what he ought to do, or what’s in his best interest right now. Let him follow his own programming; make it easy." },
      { h:"Why they swing downwind — and how to stop it", body:"Pressured elk skip step one. They’d like to come look, but every other time they closed on a sound, a blob moved, something flew at them, or there was a loud bang. So they jump to option three: <b>smell you from a safe distance</b>, without you ever knowing they were there.<br><br>The fix is to take that option away. Set up so terrain, blowdowns, a rock pile or a scree slope <b>physically blocks</b> a downwind loop — or put a <b>big open park downwind</b> so getting your scent means fully exposing themselves. Remove the ability (or the appeal), and they revert to <b>see you first</b> and come in where you can plan for them." },
      { h:"Takeaways", body:"<ul><li>Elk want to <b>see you first, hear you second, smell you third</b>.</li><li>Given the choice, they’ll engage <b>visually</b> at the first opportunity.</li><li>You’ll never make an elk do anything — you can only make the right move feel like <b>his best interest</b>.</li><li>Choose the setup and the calls so coming in to look is the natural thing to do.</li><li>When they’ve learned to skip to smell, make going downwind <b>impossible or unappealing</b>.</li></ul>" }
    ]
  },
  {
    id:"elk-psychology",
    tag:"Behavior · the psychology seminar",
    title:"Who are you actually calling to?",
    summary:"Elk are not interchangeable. Instinct, environment and individual personality spread them across a wide range, and the screaming, come-looking-for-a-fight bull everyone pictures is a thin sliver at one end. Pick a calling strategy that reaches the most of them — which means starting at the bedrock every elk has known since birth.",
    sections:[
      { h:"Why this is worth your time", body:"Chris’s premise is simple: <b>understand their behavior and account for it, and you can manipulate the situation better</b> — which makes you a more <b>efficient</b> caller. Efficiency is the whole point, because most of us don’t get thirty days in the woods each fall. We get a week. In college he’d show up Friday night, hunt Saturday and Sunday morning, and drive back for school and work.<br><br>His framing for it is the <b>valley of ten bulls</b>: you walk into an area holding ten different groups. The goal isn’t to find the one group that suits how you like to call — it’s to carry the tools to work <b>all ten</b>, however each one needs to be worked. That’s efficiency on the landscape, and it matters most exactly where it’s hardest: public, over-the-counter ground with other hunters competing for the same animals.<br><br>Psychology, in his use of the word, includes “<i>a subtle tactical action or argument used to influence another</i>.” An argument is just a discussion — heated or low-key — where you make your case and they answer. With an elk, you’re having that discussion to get him somewhere vulnerable." },
      { h:"Nothing out there is uniform", body:"Run the checklist and it falls apart fast. The units we hunt aren’t the same — different states, different habitat, different <b>predation</b> (some have wolves), wildly different <b>hunting pressure</b>, different <b>age class</b>, different <b>sex ratios</b>. The seasons aren’t the same either: an August 15 archery opener, mid-September, an early October rifle tag.<br><br>People push back on this with Chris’s own line: “<i>but you always say elk are elk.</i>” He does say it — and it’s true at one specific level, which is the next section. It just isn’t the whole story." },
      { h:"Same species, still different animals", body:"His teaching device, and it lands. Think about <b>black labs</b>. Some are raised as pups in the same stable, loving household. Some were abandoned and carry real trauma and behavioral scarring. Some are naturally high-strung and want to jump on everybody; some just want to loaf in the corner. Some lick your face off; others sit a moment and go do their own thing. Same breed.<br><br>Then horses. He had four registered quarter horses, all raised in his own household from 18 months to two years, all the same pen, same feed, same experiences, same training — every bad habit their own fault. Today one still throws his head if you reach over him: <i>fifteen years, dude, come on</i>. Another watched a crowbar bounce off his side, watched it hit the ground, and walked over like nothing happened. The mare sees the riding saddle and thinks <i>crap</i>; sees the pack saddle and starts bouncing — <i>let’s go, I don’t care where</i>.<br><br>So: same species in different situations behave differently — and <b>same species in the exact same situation still behave differently</b>. How often do we think about that while walking a mountain?" },
      { h:"Behavior rests on three things", body:"<b>1) Innate instinct.</b> The genetic hardwiring. Every elk everywhere has the same vocalizations, the same structure of communication, the same reproductive strategy, the same maternal strategy. This is why decades of reintroduction worked — Colorado’s herd was rebuilt with animals from Yellowstone, Arizona and elsewhere after market hunting wiped it out — and why you can cross state lines and still call elk effectively. <b>It’s the bedrock everything else is built on. It’s also where most hunters stop.</b><br><br><b>2) Environment and learned experience.</b> Elk outside Estes Park treat people as scenery and will calve on somebody’s back deck. Elk in Wyoming and Idaho learn there are wolves and behave accordingly. Heavily pressured elk learn <i>it must be opening day, everybody showed up</i>. And it isn’t only predators — <b>competition between elk</b> teaches too. A bull on a private Montana ranch learns he can run the country screaming his head off at no cost. A 2½- or 3½-year-old on public land, holding the first three cows of his life among bulls his own size, may work out that there’s a <b>cost to opening his mouth</b> — and decide he’s happy with the girls he’s got, thanks, and he’ll just keep quiet over here.<br><br><b>3) Individual personality.</b> The one almost nobody accounts for, and one Chris says he hadn’t really addressed until this talk. People are extroverts and introverts, conscientious or not, across a broad spectrum — all of it built on the same instinct plus the same kind of formative experience, and yet everyone still turns out different. <b>Elk are no different.</b>" },
      { h:"The curve", body:"He frames the whole thing with a plain <b>bell curve</b> — not real statistics, just a picture to think with. Roughly <b>68%</b> of the elk you randomly run into sit in the middle band; widen it and about <b>95%</b> fall inside.<br><br>Which puts the animal everybody wants — the hyper-vocal, hyper-aggressive bull who screams his head off, runs his cows in circles and comes looking for a fight — way out in a <b>thin tail</b>. He exists. He’s a tiny fraction of what’s on the mountain.<br><br>And he’s scarcer still where most of us hunt. Over-the-counter units exist because the agency is managing for <b>opportunity</b> — more hunters, more tags, more harvest — and more harvest means a <b>younger age class</b> on the landscape." },
      { h:"What the spread looks like in real elk", body:"<b>Cows.</b> Not every cow has the same <b>maternal drive</b>. Some are simply not good mothers and aren’t that interested. Others produce and successfully raise a calf every year — and they’re the ones doing <b>babysitter duty</b> in the big summer groups, two or three cows minding fifty calves while the rest of the herd feeds a couple of hundred yards off. Cows rotate through almost like shifts, but it is never all of them; a handful are just phenomenal mothers. Horses, cattle and dogs are the same. It’s a mammal thing.<br><br>Not every cow is equally <b>social</b>, either. Some want to be in the middle of the group at all times. Others you’ll find alone or in a tiny band up in the alpine, or hanging with bulls. You can argue they lost a calf, but plenty simply aren’t that gregarious — some choose big harems, some choose small groups.<br><br><b>Bulls.</b> Same spread, and two distinctions worth getting right:<ul><li><b>Dominant vs submissive by choice</b> — not from getting whipped down the hierarchy. Some bulls, when another group arrives, simply default to a lower rung. Watch long enough and you’ll see them pick that position again and again.</li><li><b>Aggressive is not the same as dominant.</b> A bull can be aggressive and still subordinate; a dominant bull can be fairly passive. A hyper-aggressive 2½-year-old with five-, six- and eight-year-olds around isn’t on his way to dominance — he’s just going to keep getting whipped, because he’s too aggressive to handle himself yet.</li><li><b>Social vs solitary.</b> Some bulls want to be in the thick of it. Some <b>mature</b> bulls never hold a harem at all — they let the younger bulls do the herding and the fighting, then wander in when a cow is ready: <i>thank you, gentlemen, I’ll take this from here, I’ll be out of your way shortly</i>. He breeds her and leaves. No interest in the melee.</li><li><b>Tolerance to disturbance.</b> Some animals shrug off real pressure and carry on. Others you bump once and they’re gone. Some of that is learned — <i>this is the trigger, I know what comes next</i> — but some animals simply don’t have it in them.</li></ul>" },
      { h:"Elk are bands, not points", body:"No elk sits on a single spot on that curve. Each one lives in a <b>band</b> — good days, bad days, and most days — exactly like us. Slept well, feeling strong, nothing bothering you, and you push the envelope a little. Bad night, something off, and you’re on the low end of your own normal.<br><br>So an elk’s ordinary disposition is a bracket, and on a given day he can bump <b>one bracket up or one down</b>. What he almost never does is jump clear across the curve — that’s a different animal, not a different day.<br><br>This is the part with teeth. Target a bull with a strategy built for who he is on a <b>good</b> day, and a low-pressure system or three other hunting parties pushing him around eight ways from Sunday will leave you calling to an animal who isn’t there anymore." },
      { h:"Two things move the curve", body:"<b>Season moves the whole curve.</b> Winter and early spring, hormones are low and there’s little going on — the entire distribution sits low and quiet. Late summer into fall it slides up as hormones rise. So before anything else, know whether you’re hunting a period when everything is ramping up, or a late rifle season when it’s all over and you’ll have to lay eyes on them and weasel them out.<br><br><b>Learned behavior moves animals within the curve.</b> In wolf country, the cost of noise is a dead calf — so animals shift toward the quiet end, and you end up with fewer that want to be vocal and more that say nothing at all. Flip it: a bull with no competition, no pressure and nothing to lose has <b>no cost</b> to bumping up his activity and vocalizing — and a real benefit, because it may bring him more cows. Same curve, different place on it." },
      { h:"So who are you calling to?", body:"You don’t get to choose. You find elk, engage them, and <i>then</i> work out who they are. So the question is which slice your strategy actually reaches.<ul><li><b>Aim at the far tail</b> — the screamer — and you will completely miss everything to the left of him. You’re fishing for a small percentage.</li><li><b>Sit passively by a meadow</b> chirping every fifteen, twenty-five, forty-five minutes and you may pick up the quiet end — but the genuinely active bull is covering ground and won’t wait around for you.</li></ul><b>Chris’s pick is the active-but-not-extreme side.</b> Set up there and you can reach that group directly, bump up to engage the hot ones when you find them, or dial down for the subdued ones. One position, most of the curve covered.<br><br>Worth saying plainly: none of this makes chasing the screamer wrong. It’s a legitimate value set, it’s the sexy version of elk hunting, and it’s what we all picture during the rut. Just know you’re hunting a sliver." },
      { h:"Forget testosterone — start at bedrock", body:"<b>Testosterone is a wild card. So is estrogen.</b> Chris doesn’t play with either. A sound that runs a bull over one day does nothing in the same spot the next.<br><br>Instead he goes to the first of the three foundations — <b>innate communication</b> — because every elk on the mountain understands it regardless of age, season, pressure or hormone state. He splits cow calling into tiers:<ul><li><b>Passive — chirps and mews.</b> You are deliberately <b>not</b> broadcasting to the landscape; you’re only talking within your own little group. Perfect for cautious, shy, keep-to-themselves animals that want to sneak in and look. A young bull may come check you out because it suits him — but you never asked him to, and a group that wants to be left alone may just quietly leave.</li><li><b>Targeted — the lost mew and the assembly mew.</b> A <b>lost mew</b> asks for a <b>response</b>, not an action. That’s why the classic complaint — “<i>he bugled back at every single call and never came in</i>” — is actually a success: he did exactly what you asked. The <b>assembly mew</b> flips it, dragging out and holding the <b>low</b> note, and says <i>come to me</i>. Get a bull answering lost mews, then switch to assembly mews, and in Chris’s words that animal is as good as dead.</li></ul>His analogy for why this works on any elk: <b>“poopy britches.”</b> Nobody says it in daily life unless they have an infant — but everybody knows exactly what it means, because we were all children once. Same for elk. A 12½-year-old bull had a mother who told him when to nurse, when to bed down, where she was and to come to her. He knows precisely what a lost mew and an assembly mew mean.<br><br>Start at bedrock and you <b>cut out the variability</b>: it doesn’t matter if they’re wolf-pressured or hunter-pressured, all young or a mixed age class, August 15 in Utah or November in Arizona with a cow tag. Then build up — more emotion, more movement, more frequency, more aggression — as the animal warrants. The reverse doesn’t work: the subdued three-quarters of the curve will never buy the hyper-aggressive approach." },
      { h:"A note on bugling", body:"Chris teaches bugling in depth, and still doesn’t open with it. His own footage is hours of calling elk in, and people notice he rarely bugles any of them in. His answer: <b>he called them in with a cow call and didn’t need a bugle.</b><br><br>He doesn’t care how a bull arrives. Nobody has ever stood over a bull that walked in silent and stopped broadside at seven steps and wished it had bugled first. And on crowded over-the-counter ground, bugling advertises you to every other hunter in the drainage — who then move in, and the elk get bumped and leave, and now everyone is looking for a new group. He’s not interested in <b>combat bull hunting</b>: hunter versus hunter, who gets to the trailhead first, who posts first. That’s a legitimate value set for the people who enjoy it. It just isn’t his — what he wants is the interaction with the animal." },
      { h:"Research helps — until it doesn’t", body:"Before the season, look up the <b>bull-to-cow ratio</b> for your unit; agency sites and the research services publish it. Fifteen bulls per hundred cows tells you there’s heavy pressure. Forty or fifty per hundred means a lot of bulls and probably a <b>diverse age class</b> — some young, some genuinely mature. That alone tells you whether a four-year-old six-point is a good bull for the unit or a giant for it.<br><br>Experience in the same country year after year helps too. But both can fail together. Chris points at Arizona: units where you wait twenty-plus years to draw and chase 350- to 400-inch, decade-old bulls were, one recent season, as dead as the worst over-the-counter ground. You couldn’t buy a bugle. It wasn’t hunting pressure — part of that country backs onto Grand Canyon National Park. It was <b>drought</b>: cow body condition going into the fall was so poor that it <b>delayed cows coming into estrus</b>, and everything stayed suppressed.<br><br>Which is exactly the argument for bedrock. Research and experience narrow the uncertainty; they don’t remove it. A strategy built on the most stable, uniform layer of elk communication is what’s left when the year goes sideways." },
      { h:"Takeaways", body:"<ul><li>Goal is <b>efficiency</b> — work whatever you find, the first time you find it. The valley of ten bulls.</li><li>Behavior sits on three layers: <b>innate instinct</b>, <b>environment and learning</b>, and <b>individual personality</b>. Most hunters only use the first.</li><li>Elk spread across a curve. The screaming, fight-picking bull is a <b>thin tail</b> — thinner still on over-the-counter ground.</li><li>Each elk lives in a <b>band</b>, not a point: good days and bad days shift him a bracket either way.</li><li><b>Season</b> moves the whole curve; <b>learned behavior</b> moves individuals within it.</li><li>Aim <b>active but not extreme</b> — from there you can reach up or dial down.</li><li><b>Skip testosterone and estrogen.</b> Both are wild cards.</li><li>Open at <b>bedrock</b>: chirps and mews to talk privately; lost mew for a <b>response</b>, assembly mew for an <b>action</b>. Every elk has known both since birth.</li><li>Build up from there — you can always add. You can’t sell a subdued bull the hyper-aggressive version.</li></ul>" }
    ]
  },
];
